Abstract

Recent data suggest that laws banning cellular phone use while driving may not change use patterns, especially among young drivers with high rates of mobile phone adoption. We examined reasons younger drivers choose or do not choose to talk on a phone while driving among a sample of young drivers ( n  =  276) with very high ownership of cellular phones (over 99%) and a very high use of cellular phones while driving (100% for those that were primary operators of an automobile). Respondents were surveyed for patterns of use, types of call, perceived risk, and motivations for use. The data were analyzed using structural equation modeling (SEM) to explore the relationships between perceived risk of the behavior, emotionality of the call, perceived importance of the call, and how often calls were initiated versus answered. The model suggests that even though people believe that talking on a cellular phone while driving is dangerous, they will tend to initiate a cellular conversation if they believe that the call is important.
Introduction

Driving distractions in vehicles have become more prevalent than ever with an increasing amount of technology finding its way into the automobile. Radios, global positioning systems, and multimedia devices can all be found in today's cars. Arguably the most detrimental technological distraction is the cellular phone. The usage of cellular phones in vehicles has been an apparent problem since they became mainstream in the consumer market. We are reminded by tragic news stories how dangerous these technologies can be, especially when a novice driver is behind the wheel. For example, the detriments of using a cellular phone while driving made its way into the public's eye in the form of an automobile crash that killed 5 teenagers in New York State (Associated Press, 2007). The driver's cell phone records indicated that text messages were both sent and received moments before the crash, and the sheriff cited text-messaging as a probable cause of the accident. The tragedy spawned many discussions, newspaper articles, and debates on whether restrictions should be placed on using telecommunication devices while driving.

The act of driving while talking on a cellular phone is a classic example of a dual-task (see Dressel and Atchley, 2008, for a recent review). Because drivers do not have the cognitive capacity to attend to both talking and driving simultaneously, what should be the primary task (driving) suffers as a result of the secondary task (communicating via a telecommunications device). Many studies have documented the effects of talking on a cellular phone while driving, and many countries and individual jurisdictions have outlawed or limited their use, yet the usage rate of the devices remains high. For example, Wogalter and Mayhorn (2005) found that 81% of their sample reported talking on a cellular phone while driving. Such a high rate of use may be explained, in part, by the low perceived risk of talking on a phone while driving. Vanlaar and Yannis (2006) reported drivers consider talking on both a handheld and hands-free cellular phone while driving to be of relatively low perceived risk and high perceived prevalence, indicating that they did not perceive what they believed to be a very common activity to be very risky. Drivers also perceive the risk of using a hand-held device to be higher relative to a hands-free equivalent (White et al., 2004) despite many studies documenting that the physical distraction of holding the phone while driving has very little effect on driving performance (McEvoy et al., 2005, Redelmeier and Tibshirani, 1997, Strayer and Johnston, 2001). Drivers also underestimate the degree of distraction imposed by the devices while they drive (Horrey et al., 2008) though there is clearly a negative effect on attentional and cognitive capacity for talking on a phone and driving (Atchley and Dressel, 2004, McKnight and McKnight, 1993, Strayer and Johnston, 2001). Indeed, drivers and legislators tend to primarily focus on physical distraction and impairment even though the distractions associated with talking on a cellular phone while driving have been shown to be as or more debilitating than driving legally drunk both in laboratory studies (Strayer et al., 2006) and epidemiological studies that examined the association of calling with accidents (McEvoy et al., 2005, Redelmeier and Tibshirani, 1997).

A critical question is why drivers continue to use these devices, even when they are illegal, or when use is legal but when drivers are aware of the risks. Laws banning the use of telecommunication devices while driving do not necessarily seem to reduce their use. A study conducted for the Insurance Institute for Highway Safety by Foss and colleagues in May of 2008 (Foss et al., 2008) looked at teen cell phone use following North Carolina's complete ban of teen cell phone use while driving in 2006. The law placed what a teen might consider to be a fairly severe penalty for being caught: a 6-month delay in obtaining a license to drive. The data showed that teens were aware of the law, which one would expect since it would have been part of their training, and were even more aware of the law than their parents. The researchers observed teen drivers at local high schools and recorded the frequency of use, before and after the law went into effect. The researchers failed to find that phone use decreased. Though other researchers have found that laws can reduce phone use while driving (Wogalter and Mayhorn, 2005), the effects have been fairly small. Younger drivers may be particularly resistant to change since they tend to be the heaviest adopters new telecommunications technologies (Lee, 2007).

The current question is what motivates a driver to continue using these devices. If a driver knows there is risk, do they modulate their use or are their factors that outweigh perceived risk and lead to cellular phone use while driving? We are especially interested in younger drivers, since this group has a very high rate of adoption of the devices and literally represents the future of the roadways (Lee, 2007, Olsen et al., 2005). To this end, we conducted a study in which we examined reasons for use among younger drivers with a high rate of adoption of cellular phones. Participants were asked questions regarding their perceived risk, frequency, emotionality, and importance of differing cellular phone conversation types that they have while driving. Participants also reported how likely they are to either answer or initiate a cellular phone conversation while driving and how likely they are to text message while driving. The data were analyzed using statistical modeling to quantify, as best possible, relationships among variables which might motivate a user to make or take calls while driving, such as risk perception and perception of the importance of making a call.

The focus of this study was to explore how often people talk on a cellular phone while driving, and what factors influence them to do this. In the case of cellular phones, most people understand that talking while driving is risky. However, they still tend to pick up the phone. White et al. (2007) proposed that perceptions of benefit among other things are vital to understanding people's risk perceptions. They found what they call comparative utility. This means that cellular phone users tend to believe that the benefits of the technology are larger for them than for others. This finding is similar to optimism bias, or comparative optimism, where people believe that it is safer for them to talk on the cellular phone while driving than for others to talk on the cellular phone while driving. We believe that perceived importance is a significant factor that a person either explicitly or implicitly considers when they are deciding whether or not to initiate or answer a cellular phone call while driving. Therefore, we predicted that perceived importance is a significant predictor of answering, initiating, and total reported frequency of talking on a cellular phone while driving. We also predicted that perceived risk would be a significant predictor of likelihood of answering, initiating, and total reported frequency of talking on a cellular phone while driving. Additionally, based upon pilot work, we included questions about the emotionality of conversations to examine if drivers tend to try to avoid initiating emotional conversations while driving because they feel those conversations are overly distracting. Furthermore, we hypothesized that perceived importance of a cellular phone call would cancel out any effects that perceived risk has on reducing the likelihood of answering, initiating, and talking on a cellular phone while driving in general.
